==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|médiocrité}}, from {{der|en|la|mediocritās}}, from {{m|la|mediocris}}; {{surf|en|mediocre|-ity|nocap=1}}.
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/miː.dɪˈɒk.ɹɪ.ti/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-mediocrity.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/mi.dɪˈɑk.ɹɪ.ti/|[mi.dɪˈɑk.ɹɪ.ɾi]|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The condition of being [[mediocre]]; having only an [[average]] [[degree]] of [[quality]], [[skill]]s etc.; no better than standard.
#: {{syn|en|middlingness}}
#: {{cot|en|midness<qq:denoting low quality in 21st-century slang>|;|inadequacy|insufficiency|poorness|;|excellence}}
#: {{ux|en|Flexibility is good, but a tolerance for '''mediocrity''' carried far enough impairs operational capacity.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=28 March 2021|author=Phil McNulty|title=Albania 0-2 England|work=BBC Sport|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/56472933|passage=England captain {{w|Harry Kane}} lifted the '''mediocrity''' of an attritional first half on a slow surface when he scored his 33rd goal for his country, a superbly guided diving header from {{w|Luke Shaw}}'s cross seven minutes before the interval.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A person with mediocre [[ability|abilities]] or [[achievement]]s.
#: {{collocation|en|populated with '''mediocrities'''}}
#: {{collocation|en|surrounded by '''mediocrities'''}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} The quality of being [[intermediate]] between two extremes; a [[mean]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A middle course of action; [[moderation]], [[balance]].
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd}}, New York Review Books 2001, p.273:
#*: In adversity I wish for prosperity, and in prosperity I am afraid of adversity. What '''mediocrity''' may be found?

